1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 e6 4. O-O Nge7 5. c3 d5 6. exd5 Qxd5 7. Re1 a6 
    ( 7. ... g6 8. b4 $5 Bg7 9. Bb2 {was played in a rapidplay game 
    Kasparov-Kramnik,  Paris 1992, with White winning fairly quickly.} )
8. Ba4 b5 9. Bc2 Bb7 10. a4 {[diagram]} 10. ... b4 
    ( 10. ... Ng6 {has been played here previously, when  White has} 11. 
    axb5 axb5 12. Rxa8+ Bxa8 13. Na3 {and a considerable  initiative. 
    After the text, White plays enterprisingly to establish a  significant
    advantage in development.} )
11. d4 Rd8 12. c4 $1 Qh5 
    ( 12. ... Qxc4 $4 {loses the queen (or, at least, a lot of material) 
    to} 13. Bb3 )
13. d5 exd5 {[Black is more or less obliged to accept Adams's pawn  
sacrifice, leaving him vulnerable on the open e-file and with little  
prospect of moving his e7 knight or f8 bishop.]} 14. Nbd2 dxc4 
    ( 14. ... f6 $5 {The computer suggests , though the position still 
    looks difficult for  Black after} 15. cxd5 Nd4 16. Nxd4 Qxd1 17. Bxd1 
    cxd4 18. d6 Rxd6 19. Nc4 Rd5 20. Bf4 {, etc, and the e-file problem 
    has yet to be resolved.} )
15. Qe2 Na5 16. Ne4 $1 
    ( 16. Nxc4 {may also be good.} )
16. ... Nb3 17. Bxb3 
    ( 17. Bg5 $1 {The immediate is also very strong. If} 17. ... Nxa1 18. 
    Bxe7 Bxe7 19. Nf6+ $1 {and mate next move.} )
17. ... cxb3 18. Bg5 $1 f6 $2 
    ( 18. ... Bxe4 {Black falls on his sword.} 19. Qxe4 Qg6 20. Qc4 {was a
    better defence for Black but  would most probably have turned into a 
    typical Adams grind, as he has more than enough positional 
    compensation for the two pawns.} )
19. Bxf6 Kf7 20. Ne5+ 1-0

