1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. O-O Nxe4 5. d4 Be7 
    ( 5. ... Nd6 {is 'the real  Kramnik', as used to wrest the world title
    from Garry Kasparov in 2000.} )
6. Qe2 Nd6 7. Bxc6 bxc6 
    ( 7. ... dxc6 $2 {is rarely played as it leads to big  trouble after} 
    8. dxe5 Nf5 9. Rd1 Bd7 {(only move)} 10. e6 $1 fxe6 11. Ne5 Bd6 12. 
    Qh5+ {, etc.} )
8. dxe5 Nb7 9. c4 
    ( 9. Nc3 {is more frequently seen.} )
9. ... O-O 10. Nc3 f6 
    ( 10. ... Re8 {Nigel Short  chose  against Hou Yifan at  Corus 2008 
    but lost quite quickly.} )
11. Re1 fxe5 12. Qxe5 Bf6 13. Qg3 {A  new move, where 13 Qh5 had 
previously been preferred.} 13. ... Nc5 14. Bg5 {[diagram]} 14. ... Nd3 $2
    ( 14. ... d6 {Black regretted this move, which gets  him into trouble.
    is perhaps a better alternative, keeping a wary eye on  White's 
    kingside build-up and  continuing with development.} )
15. Re3 $1 {David Howell had originally expected 15 Re2 but when Black 
started to  spend a long time on his reply, he suddenly became aware of 
this more  forceful response.} 15. ... Nxb2 {There is  little else for it 
but to follow  through with this capture.} 16. Rae1 Bxg5 $6 
    ( 16. ... Ba6 {Mickey Adams said  he had expected when he might have 
    replied} 17. Ne4 {with some play for the pawn, though there is chess 
    left in the position.} )
17. Nxg5 Qf6 {Anything and 18 Re7 would be unanswerable.} 18. Rf3 Qd8 19. 
Nce4 
    ( 19. Qe5 {White has  a choice of good moves. Another is , which 
    craftily closes on the black  knight, e.g.} 19. ... Nxc4 20. Rxf8+ 
    Qxf8 21. Qe4 {with twin threats of Qxh7  mate and Qxc4.} )
19. ... Ba6 20. Nxh7 $1 
    ( 20. Rxf8+ {is also very good, e.g.} 20. ... Qxf8 21. Nxh7 $1 Kxh7 
    22. Ng5+ Kg8 23. Qh3 $1 {and it's all over.} )
20. ... Rxf3 21. gxf3 Kxh7 22. Ng5+ Kg8 23. Qh4 Bxc4 24. Qh7+ Kf8 25. Re5 
Be6 26. Qh8+ Ke7 27. Qxg7+ Kd6 28. Ne4# 1-0

